    topic: "Studying abroad: a lonely first term",
    passage: "Dilnoza Karimova left Samarkand for Manchester in September, expecting that three years of studying civil engineering abroad would be the hardest challenge of her life. Looking back after her first term, she admits that the academic work was not the problem at all. Her English had been good enough to pass the entrance test with a comfortable score of 6.5, and she had always been the kind of student who finished assignments well before the deadline. What really surprised her was how lonely the first weeks felt, even though she was surrounded by thousands of people.\n\nThe university had arranged a welcome week, but Dilnoza found the crowded parties exhausting and left early on the second night. Her flatmates, a Nigerian medical student and two British first-years, were friendly but spent most evenings out. It was only when she joined the university's cycling club, almost by accident, that things began to change. She had never owned a bicycle in Uzbekistan, and she describes her first ride, which lasted only forty minutes, as the most embarrassing experience of the term. Nevertheless, the other members were patient, and by November she was joining the Saturday trips of around thirty kilometres.\n\nMoney was another worry. Her parents pay the tuition fees, which amount to nearly fifteen thousand pounds a year, but she is responsible for her own rent and food. A friend advised her to look for a part-time job, yet her student visa allows her to work no more than twenty hours a week during term, so she chose a modest eight hours in the library. The wages are low, but she says the real benefit is that colleagues correct her pronunciation without making her feel foolish.\n\nAlthough she misses her family terribly, Dilnoza does not regret her decision. She phones her mother every Sunday, and they usually talk for an hour, mostly about food. Her younger brother has recently announced that he wants to study in Germany, which she finds both flattering and slightly worrying, since she knows how many difficulties he will face. Her advice to him is simple: do not expect the country to adapt to you. Learn the small habits, such as queueing politely and saying thank you to the bus driver, and the big problems will become much easier to solve.\n\nIf she could change one thing, she says, it would be her arrival date. She came only two days before lectures began, so she had no time to recover from the journey, find the shops or understand the bus system. Next year, she plans to arrive at least a fortnight early, and she is already encouraging new students from Samarkand to do the same.",
