    topic: "A student loan and a long repayment",
    passage: "When Harriet Bellamy graduated from a university in Leeds, she owed the government a little over twenty-seven thousand pounds. For years she had avoided looking at the figure, partly because her father, who had paid for his own degree with a summer job in a factory, kept describing student loans as a mortgage on your future. Harriet now thinks that description is misleading, though she admits she did not understand the system when she signed the forms at eighteen.\n\nThe first surprise was that repayments do not begin immediately after graduation. They start in the April following the course, and only if the graduate earns more than twenty-five thousand pounds a year. Anyone earning less pays nothing, however large the original loan was. Above that threshold, the borrower pays nine per cent of the extra income, so Harriet, who earns thirty-one thousand pounds as a marketing assistant, hands over only about forty-five pounds a month. She points out that this amount is deducted automatically from her salary, so she never feels it as a separate bill.\n\nThe second surprise concerned interest. Harriet had assumed that the balance would shrink slowly each month. In reality, interest is added so quickly that someone on her salary will probably never reduce the original sum. Whatever remains is cancelled after thirty years. Critics call this unfair because it means most graduates will be paying for three decades, and Harriet partly agrees. Nevertheless, she argues that the system behaves more like a graduate tax than like a normal debt, because the payments rise and fall with income and disappear if she loses her job.\n\nHer brother Tom is less relaxed. He is studying medicine, a course that lasts five years, so his loan will be considerably larger than hers, and he fears that it will make it harder to obtain a mortgage. Harriet has tried to reassure him by explaining that lenders only look at the monthly deduction, not at the total, but he remains unconvinced. Their father, meanwhile, has offered to pay off part of Tom's loan from his retirement savings, an idea both children have firmly rejected. Harriet believes it would be a waste, because the extra money would simply be cancelled anyway if Tom never earns enough to clear the balance.\n\nLooking ahead, Harriet's main concern is not the loan itself but the choice between saving and repaying early. A colleague recently suggested she should make voluntary payments, but she decided against it. She would rather put the same money into a savings account for a deposit on a flat, which she hopes to buy before she turns thirty.",
