    topic: "Finding a first flat",
    passage: "Kamila Yusupova was twenty-four when she finally decided to leave her parents' house in Tashkent and rent a flat of her own, and she is now the first to say that she underestimated almost everything. She had imagined a pleasant weekend of looking at bright rooms, shaking hands with friendly landlords and signing a contract by Monday. Instead, the search took nearly six weeks, and she viewed nine flats before choosing one.\n\nThe first flat she saw looked perfect in the photographs. In reality, there was a dark patch of damp above the bed, which the owner described as just a little condensation. Kamila, who had been warned by an older cousin to trust her nose rather than the advertisement, said no politely and left. The fourth flat was cheap but so far from her office that the journey would have taken ninety minutes each way. The seventh was close to the metro, yet the landlord insisted on a contract of three years, which she considered far too long for someone who might change jobs.\n\nThe flat she eventually chose belongs to a retired engineer called Mr Okafor, who lives abroad and manages everything through his sister. The rent is four hundred and fifty dollars a month, and utilities are not included, something she only noticed after reading the contract twice. She also paid a deposit equal to one month's rent, which the owner promised to return at the end of the year if nothing was damaged. Her friend Jonas, who works as a nurse, shares the flat and pays half.\n\nLooking back, Kamila believes her biggest mistake was not photographing the flat on the day she moved in. A month later, she noticed a long scratch on the kitchen table and a loose door handle in the bathroom, and she spent an anxious evening wondering whether she would be blamed. Fortunately, Mr Okafor's sister was understanding, but Kamila has since learned that many tenants lose a large part of their deposit for exactly this reason. She now advises friends to write a detailed list of every defect and send it to the owner by message so that there is a record.\n\nThe other lesson was about money. Her salary of nine hundred dollars a month seemed generous until she added rent, electricity, internet and food. After the first month, she had only forty dollars left for everything else. She has therefore started to cook at home rather than eating out, and she has cancelled a gym membership she hardly ever used. Despite the stress, she would not return to her parents' house. She says the feeling of unlocking her own door every evening is worth every complicated negotiation.",
