    topic: "A mentor who asked questions",
    passage: "Joanna Reyes was twenty-six, three years into her first job at an architecture firm in Manchester, when her manager suggested that she should be paired with a mentor. She was suspicious. In her experience, mentoring schemes consisted of awkward lunches and vague promises, and she had a great deal of real work waiting on her desk. Nevertheless, she agreed, and she now regards that decision as one of the most important of her career.\n\nHer mentor was Mr Hassan, a senior partner who had been designing hospitals for almost thirty years. They met on the first Thursday of every month, always for exactly one hour, and always in his small office rather than in a restaurant. At first, Joanna was frustrated, because Mr Hassan almost never gave a direct answer. When she asked whether she should apply for a promotion, he replied by asking what she hoped to learn in the new role. When she complained that a colleague was taking credit for her ideas, he asked what she had already done about it. She left the early meetings feeling that she had learned nothing.\n\nThe turning point came about eighteen months later, when Joanna nearly resigned. A major project had been taken away from her after a disagreement with a client, and she felt humiliated. She arrived at the Thursday meeting with her letter of resignation in her bag, expecting sympathy. Instead, Mr Hassan listened without interrupting for twenty minutes and then asked a single question: what would she advise a younger colleague in exactly her position? Joanna says that she sat in silence for almost a minute before admitting that she would tell the colleague to stay and learn from the mistake. She never handed in the letter.\n\nOver time, she came to understand his method. By refusing to give instructions, he forced her to examine her own reasoning, and the solutions she found herself were far easier to carry out than any advice he could have offered. She also noticed that he never criticised other people in front of her, a habit she has deliberately copied. Hardly anyone in the firm knew that he had been rejected by three universities before he was finally accepted onto an architecture course.\n\nThree years after they first met, Mr Hassan retired, and Joanna was promoted to project leader. She now mentors two junior architects, and she is trying to resist the temptation to hand out quick answers. Her first mentee, a talkative young man called Ilyas, complained after two meetings that she was being unhelpful. Joanna smiled and asked him what he thought a good mentor should do. She admits it was the most satisfying moment of her week.",
