    topic: "University or apprenticeship?",
    passage: "Callum and Ethan Pritchard are identical twins, and for most of their childhood they were treated as a single unit. At eighteen, however, they made opposite decisions. Callum went to university to study business, while Ethan, to the surprise of his teachers, chose a four-year apprenticeship at an engineering company near Sheffield. Now, five years later, they sometimes argue about whose choice was wiser, and the truth is that neither of them can answer that question with any confidence.\n\nOn paper, Ethan was ahead from the start. His employer paid him around eighteen thousand pounds a year from the first month, and the company covered the cost of his training. By twenty-one, he had a recognised qualification, a driving licence funded partly by his salary and no debt at all. Callum, meanwhile, was living in a shared house in Bristol and eating an enormous quantity of pasta. His course fees were financed by a loan, and the part-time job in a bar earned him only enough to pay the rent.\n\nYet the picture is not as simple as it appears. Callum's degree opened doors that were firmly closed to his brother, at least at first. He was offered a graduate position in a consultancy, with a starting salary of thirty thousand pounds, and within two years he had been promoted. Ethan, though skilled, discovered that many senior roles in his sector asked for a degree, which he now has to complete in the evenings, at his own expense. He describes this as the hidden cost of the apprenticeship route, a cost nobody had mentioned at the careers fair.\n\nThe twins also disagree about the less measurable benefits. Callum insists that university taught him to argue, to question assumptions and to work with people he would never have met otherwise. Ethan counters that his workplace gave him the same things, together with something university could not: the discipline of being responsible for expensive equipment from the age of eighteen. He also points out that several of Callum's friends have graduated without finding any job in their field.\n\nTheir mother, who has listened patiently to this debate for years, believes that both of them are exaggerating. In her view, the quality of the particular course or employer matters far more than the type of path. She has seen a talented nephew waste three years on a poor degree, and a careless neighbour's son fail to finish an excellent apprenticeship. What counts, she says, is the willingness to learn, not the name of the institution.\n\nThe twins have at least agreed on one point. If either of them has children, they will encourage them to visit at least five workplaces and five universities before deciding anything.",
