    topic: "Moving in with a partner",
    passage: "After two years together, Dani Ortega and Zafar Mirzaev decided to move into a flat in Madrid, and everyone they knew assured them that it was the moment when the real relationship began. Friends warned about dirty dishes, relatives about money, and Zafar's grandmother, rather mysteriously, about the dangers of sharing a bathroom. Dani smiled politely at all of them, secretly convinced that two sensible adults who had never quarrelled seriously would manage without any help.\n\nThey were wrong, though not in the way they had expected. The first argument was not about dishes at all. It was about a sofa. Dani wanted a large grey one that she had seen in a shop, while Zafar, who had been living in a rented room with almost no furniture, insisted that they should not spend six hundred euros on something they could find second-hand. Neither of them was being unreasonable, and that was precisely what made the discussion so difficult. In the end, they bought a used leather sofa for one hundred and eighty euros, which both now agree is rather uncomfortable.\n\nMoney proved to be a more serious subject. Dani earns about two thousand euros a month as a graphic designer, whereas Zafar, who is a junior teacher, earns around twelve hundred. Dividing the rent equally would have left him with almost nothing, yet Dani felt uneasy about paying more simply because she earned more. After several evenings with a calculator, they agreed to split shared costs in proportion to their incomes, which meant that she pays sixty per cent and he pays forty. They keep their personal accounts separate and have opened a joint account only for rent, bills and food.\n\nThe habit that made the biggest difference was an unexpected one. Every Sunday evening, they sit down for a short conversation, about twenty minutes, in which each person may raise one complaint and one thing they appreciated during the week. At first, Zafar thought it was childish. Now he admits that it prevents small irritations from growing into major quarrels, such as the matter of the bathroom light, which he leaves on and which she turns off, sometimes with him inside.\n\nLooking back, Dani says that the best preparation would have been to stay for a month at each other's places before signing a contract. She also thinks that couples underestimate how much they need time alone, so she has deliberately arranged a small desk in the bedroom where she can work in peace. Zafar, for his part, has started to enjoy the evening cooking, though he insists that this does not mean he will ever wash up willingly. Their conclusion is modest: living together is not a test of love, but it is certainly a test of habits.",
